subject:"Read IMG and lock MAIN. The multi-view images in IMG define only the same MAIN; do not interpret them as multiple people, clones, stand-ins, or characters from different timelines. There is only one visible subject throughout the entire video: MAIN. Keep FACE/HAIR/BODY/COSTUME/ACC consistent with the character sheet. The white background, column layout, annotation lines, and typography belong only to the reference image and do not enter the MV world. Do not pre-assume extra gender traits, age appearance, hair color, hairstyle, body type, or clothing category. MAIN's identity is determined by IMG; the following text only controls action, cinematography, space, optical presentation, and post-production media, not character redesign."
generation:"Generate a 30-second, Seedance 2.5, 720p, 24fps, single continuous take experimental high-density dance MV. The first frame to the last frame belongs to the same continuous camera trajectory; do not use hard cuts, jump cuts, smash cuts, independent B-roll, or sudden camera resets. MAIN continuously sings and dances from 0s to 30s and is the only visual center. The camera may perform high-speed orbit, optical-axis roll, crane, dolly, overhead arc, low-angle lateral tracking, reverse track, and foreground threading around MAIN, but all motion must connect into one continuous 3D spline. Stage changes occur behind MAIN, beneath MAIN, around MAIN, and within the depth layers; the space folds, unfolds, translates, and reorganizes like a living architecture. Any scene change must be understood as continuous spatial transformation, not editing."
vocal:"Original English dance-music lead; lyrics unspecified, original non-quoted phrases; clear lip sync; crisp consonants; energetic hooks; rhythmic spoken-sung accents; bright upper register + sustained notes; no prompt reading. MAIN continuously sings and dances from 0s onward; singing never stops. During rapid full-body movement, mouth readability may briefly shrink, but a clear face/lip-sync read must reappear every 0.8-1.2s."
music:"155 BPM maximal electronic dance track from frame one; no intro, no breakdown, no silence. 0-10s=electro-house/dance-pop drive, huge four-on-floor kick, rolling sub, supersaw walls, rave stabs, bright arps, metallic transient layers. 10-20s=electro-funk/broken-club articulation while BPM remains 155, tighter syncopated percussion, clipped bass, dry electronic hits, stop-start accents, short gated synths. 20-30s=hyper-club/rave climax, denser kick+sub, sharper hats, wide supersaws, chopped vocal FX, rising choir-like synth layers, stronger stereo motion and larger impacts. Music foreground and loud; lead vocal foremost, drums second, bass/sub thick, synth walls wide. 10s and 20s are arrangement pivots, not pauses. Starting at 27s, further increase high-hat subdivision, chopped FX, risers, and sub pulse; at 29.7s reach maximum sonic density without fading early."
goal:"30-second continuous one-take dance MV. The visual goal is not a standard stage MV, but to compress cinematography, dance, architecture, materiality, color, and media history into one continuous shot. Five rhythmic layers run in parallel without resetting each other: A = MAIN's body movement is absolutely continuous from 0-30s; B = one micro display mutation may occur every 0.05s; C = effect family, dominant color, or media tendency may change every 0.10s; D = the camera does not cut, and moves continuously along the 3D spline with one dominant movement phrase about every 1.5-2.5s; E = from 0-27s, one macro environment transformation completes about every 1.5s, and from 27-30s the background increases to one background visual state mutation every 0.10s. B/C are only display-layer and background-layer beats; the model must not interpret them as camera cuts, character redraws, or action resets."
one_take_lock:"The entire piece is a single continuous take. Hard cuts, smash cuts, jump cuts, teleportation, black-frame cuts, white-frame cuts, insert shots, independent B-roll, and sudden subject switches are forbidden. Environmental transitions may only occur through continuous camera motion: foreground wipes across the lens, LED frames passing through, prism refraction sweeping by, brief pillar occlusion, light-curtain refraction, background geometry folding, wall sliding, floor material flow, continuous dissolution of spatial volume, lighting color-temperature handoff, and projection surfaces being covered by another medium. Occlusions must be very brief; before and after any occlusion, MAIN's body orientation, footwork, arm trajectory, and camera motion vector must remain continuous and consistent. MAIN must not leave the frame for more than an extremely brief moment; by default MAIN stays within the central ±15% of the frame, and during extreme orbit may briefly move to the left or right third, but must return to center composition within 0.3s."

